messages:
anya: quick heads-up — I'm hitting a flaky case in the JS SDK when streaming SSE after a token refresh. partial JSON objects end up getting concatenated across reconnects and our incremental parser chokes. repro steps below.

sam: ugh. can you paste a minimal repro? browser or node?

anya: browser + service worker + our fetch proxy. sequence: 1) token expires, 2) sdk does auth refresh, 3) reconnect to SSE, 4) server sends a chunk boundary that contains half of a JSON object then a keepalive then rest of object — client sees them as one long chunk on reconnect and we try to JSON.parse -> SyntaxError.

mike: do you have raw bytes/logs? sometimes the TextDecoder/stream framing differs across UA. also check if the reconnect uses connection: close vs keep-alive.

anya: pasted snippet of the eventstream arriving (simplified):
```
data: {"id":1,"text":"first part


```
then on reconnect:
```
data: continuing part","meta":{...}}

```
so we get two data events that, when concatenated, form a valid JSON but our incremental parser expects each `data:` chunk to be a full JSON object.

sam: ok — two orthogonal problems: 1) assumption each SSE payload == one JSON object, 2) backpressure: when consumer slower than producer, browser buffers can merge frames. we should handle both.

mike: fixes that helped in other libs: a) use a small (bounded) buffer and an incremental streaming JSON parser (e.g., clarinet / oboe-style) to emit full objects when complete; b) keep a safe delimiter wrapper from server (e.g., `data: <len>\n` or newline-separated ndjson) so reconnect can resume boundaries; c) on reconnect, prefer to reset parser state only after a short grace window so partial fragments can be recovered if server retries.

anya: note: server cannot guarantee chunk boundaries across TCP reconnects. we need client resilient. leaning toward incremental parser + optimistic merge on very small trailing fragments.

sam: backpressure side: if consumer is slow (e.g., UI rendering large tokens), node/browser will buffer. in node you can tune highWaterMark on the socket stream; in browser we can implement an internal queue with max size and drop/compact old tokens or pause reading by closing the reader until consumer drains. the Fetch ReadableStream API supports cancel() and by default you can't throttle incoming bytes — but you can `reader.read()` at controlled pace.

anya: here's a patch idea (pseudo):
```
// read eventstream at chunk level
const reader = res.body.getReader();
let leftover = '';
async function pump() {
  while (true) {
    const {value, done} = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    leftover += decoder.decode(value, {stream: true});
    // try incremental parse: find `\n\n` delimiter between events
    let idx;
    while ((idx = leftover.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
      const raw = leftover.slice(0, idx);
      leftover = leftover.slice(idx + 2);
      // extract `data:` lines, join, attempt parse with try/catch
      const jsonText = extractDataPayload(raw);
      try { handleJSON(JSON.parse(jsonText)); }
      catch (e) { // buffer tiny tail or send to incremental parser }
    }
    // if leftover grows too big -> apply backpressure strategy
    if (leftover.length > 64_000) {
      // pause: optionally cancel reader and reconnect with resume token
      await throttleConsumer();
    }
  }
}
```

mike: looks reasonable. one more: add a tiny checksum or sequence id per SSE message so if reconnect merges two halves you can detect ordering and dedupe.

anya: good call. i can try implementing clarinet first then fallback to JSON.parse with a compacting buffer. will also add a telemetry metric for "partial-fragment-recoveries".

